  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">From waste to wetlands</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Plastic pollution is a pressing environmental issue, particularly in Southeast Asia, where rivers are major conduits of plastic waste to the oceans. Vietnam, with its extensive network of rivers and growing environmental challenges, faces a significant threat to its aquatic ecosystems. In addition to plastic pollution, it was noticed that the quality of specific water resources is being affected by pollution, which primarily stems from residential and business activities.Given the alarming levels of pollution, an effective solution is needed to improve the water quality. A potential strategy is the implementation of a floating garden model, which can serve as a wetland, designed for water filtration. This approach could help mitigate the impact of pollutants by using natural systems to absorb and filter contaminants, thereby improving the overall health of the ecosystem.This program is designed to engage young professionals and students (aged 18-30) in environmental innovation. Through online workshops, participants will gain skills in addressing plastic pollution. The program will culminate in a competition where participants design floating wetland systems made from plastic waste, fostering collaboration between private sector businesses and environmental initiatives to expand sustainable solutions and market potential. The participants are supported by a team of experts and business developers.</narrative>
  </description>
